{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "prepare": {
    "face": {
      "extract": "TASK — FACE EXTRACTION:\nCreate one photorealistic head-and-shoulders identity reference of the same person.\n\nREFERENCE ROLE:\n<image1> = FACIAL IDENTITY AND HAIR ONLY.\nIgnore its clothing, body build, pose, environment and lighting.\n\nIDENTITY ACCURACY:\n- Preserve facial structure, eye shape and spacing, nose, lips, jaw, apparent age, skin tone and distinguishing features.\n- Preserve hairline, hairstyle and hair color; keep natural skin texture.\n- Do not beautify, average identities, change age or substitute a generic fashion-model face.\n\nFRAMING AND STAGING:\n- One neutral frontal portrait; entire head, hair and chin inside the frame.\n- Face large and readable, shoulders only, natural perspective and relaxed expression.\n- Use a plain neutral shirt; remove unrelated source clothing and accessories from the portrait.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne clean identity portrait with realistic eyes, hair and skin. No full-body view, duplicate person, panels, captions, logo or readable text.",
      "generate": "TASK — FACE GENERATION:\nCreate one original adult character identity from the supplied description.\n\nINPUT AUTHORITY:\nUse the written identity description and additional instructions. No reference image is supplied for this task.\n\nIDENTITY DESIGN:\n- Follow the requested facial structure, apparent adult age, skin tone, distinguishing features, hairstyle and hair color.\n- Keep realistic facial proportions, natural skin texture and coherent eyes and teeth.\n- Do not substitute a generic beauty-filter face for specified features.\n\nFRAMING AND STAGING:\nOne frontal head-and-shoulders portrait, relaxed neutral expression, entire head and chin visible, plain neutral shirt.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne photorealistic identity portrait. No full-body image, multiple people, panels, labels or readable text.",
      "negative": "IDENTITY ERRORS:\ndifferent identity, blended faces, altered facial proportions, changed age, generic face, changed hairline\n\nANATOMY AND SURFACE ERRORS:\nasymmetrical eyes, malformed eyes, malformed teeth, warped jaw, plastic skin, over-retouched skin, waxy skin\n\nFRAMING AND PRESENTATION ERRORS:\ncropped forehead, cropped chin, full-body framing, duplicate person, collage, panels, harsh shadows, color cast, blur, text, watermark"
    },
    "body": {
      "extract": "TASK — BODY PROPORTION EXTRACTION:\nCreate one photorealistic neck-to-feet physique reference from the supplied image.\n\nREFERENCE ROLE:\n<image1> = VISIBLE BODY BUILD AND RELATIVE PROPORTIONS ONLY.\nThis preparation image documents physique; its clothing is not the wardrobe for final character assembly.\n\nPROPORTION ACCURACY:\n- Preserve shoulder width, ribcage width, chest depth, breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage and waist.\n- Preserve torso length, abdomen fullness, waist-to-hip relationship, pelvis width, buttocks and thigh volume, muscularity and limb lengths.\n- A fuller bust stays fuller and a smaller bust stays smaller; do not normalize either toward an average build.\n- Do not slim, elongate or idealize the body. Distinguish visible anatomy from loose fabric; do not invent BMI, cup size, height, weight or hidden measurements.\n\nSOURCE COVERAGE:\n- Preserve the source body surface and clothing coverage: do not add garments, remove existing garments, or invent accessories.\n- Keep the source's visible body relationships without adding unrelated garments or redesigning its outfit.\n- This coverage rule applies only to this preparation image; assembly takes wardrobe from its separate clothing instructions.\n\nFRAMING AND STAGING:\n- One neck-to-feet study; exclude the head and facial identity.\n- Show complete arms, separated relaxed hands and both feet, with a neutral standing posture.\n- Level camera, natural perspective, no wide-angle stretching or measurement annotations.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne proportion study preserving the source build. No face portrait, turnaround, collage, labels or readable text.",
      "generate": "TASK — BODY PROPORTION GENERATION:\nCreate one original adult neck-to-feet physique study from the body description.\n\nINPUT AUTHORITY:\nUse the written physique and additional instructions. No reference image is supplied for this task.\n\nPROPORTION TARGETS:\n- Follow specified shoulder/ribcage width, chest depth and breast volume relative to waist, abdomen, hips, buttocks, thighs and limb lengths.\n- Keep fuller or smaller proportions as described; do not normalize to a generic average build.\n- No numerical BMI or hidden-measurement claims.\n\nCOVERAGE AND FRAMING:\n- Plain close-fitting neutral exercise clothing used only for this proportion study, not as the final character wardrobe.\n- Exclude the head; show complete arms, relaxed separated hands and both feet.\n- Neutral standing posture, level camera and natural perspective.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne photorealistic physique reference. No identity portrait, panels, labels or readable text.",
      "negative": "PROPORTION ERRORS:\nslimming, elongated torso, shortened legs, distorted pelvis, unrequested breast reduction or enlargement, flattened reference bust, generic averaged physique, altered waist-to-hip relationship\n\nANATOMY AND FRAMING ERRORS:\nvisible face, head portrait, copied facial identity, disconnected arms, missing limbs, extra limbs, fused fingers, cropped feet, wide-angle distortion\n\nTASK AND PRESENTATION ERRORS:\nunrequested clothing coverage changes during extraction, invented accessories, duplicate body, collage, panels, plastic skin, harsh shadows, text, measurement labels, watermark"
    },
    "clothing": {
      "extract": "TASK — CLOTHING EXTRACTION AND OUTFIT COMPLETION:\nCreate one photorealistic garment-only flat lay containing a complete coordinated outfit for a full-body character preview. Preserve the source items first, then design only the missing pieces.\n\nREFERENCE ROLE:\n<image1> = VISIBLE GARMENTS, HEADWEAR, FOOTWEAR AND WEARABLE ACCESSORIES ONLY.\nDo not copy the wearer, anatomy, pose, background, lighting or color grading.\n\nVISIBLE INVENTORY:\n- Identify every separate visible wearable item before arranging the flat lay, including small or partly occluded inner garments. Keep one of each.\n- Preserve visible bras, lace tops, straps and other inner garments when removing the wearer; they are clothing, not anatomy to erase.\n- Treat visible source garments as fixed design references. A cropped upper-body image specifies only part of the wardrobe.\n- Missing pieces are coordinated design additions, not recovered evidence of what the source person wore.\n\nCOMPLETION POLICY — EDIT THIS SECTION TO CUSTOMIZE:\n- Complete the outfit before character assembly. Keep all visible source items unchanged.\n- Follow any explicit completion inventory below; otherwise choose one coherent lower-body garment and one matching pair of shoes or boots appropriate to the outfit style, palette and season.\n- Include matching underwear and socks when appropriate to the outfit, and a belt when specified or needed by the chosen trousers. Do not add redundant alternatives or unnecessary accessories.\n- A dress or other existing full-length garment can already cover the lower body; do not automatically add trousers. Explicit barefoot or underwear-only outfit instructions take precedence.\n- Show every retained and newly designed piece separately in this flat lay, including underwear that will later be hidden beneath outer clothing.\n- Add only missing categories. Never replace a visible bra with briefs, add an unrelated shirt over it, or redesign the existing jacket.\n\nQUANTITIES — ONE OUTFIT FOR ONE PERSON:\n- Build one combined inventory of retained source items and designed additions. A mention elsewhere describes that same item; it never requests another copy.\n- Each single garment or accessory appears exactly once unless an explicit quantity says otherwise. Briefs are one garment; trousers are one intact garment with two legs.\n- A footwear pair means exactly two individual shoes/boots total: one left and one right, together in one area. Do not add a lone shoe beside a pair. Apply the same left/right count to requested socks or gloves.\n- Show only one view of each physical item. No front/back duplicates, alternate designs, color variants, extra sizes or repeated product shots.\n- When an explicit inventory is supplied, it is the complete list: add nothing outside it. Leave unused space white instead of repeating items to fill it.\n\nGARMENT ACCURACY:\n- Preserve garment category, silhouette, cut, intended fit, length, layers and open/closed fastening state.\n- Preserve local colors, fabric/material differences, patterns, seams, stitching, buttons, zippers, pockets, straps and visible wear.\n- Keep an open jacket as one intact outer garment and its inner garment separate; never split jacket panels into extra shirts.\n- Keep bras as chest garments, never briefs. Preserve shoe type, color, material and heel shape when footwear is visible.\n- Keep each garment once, fully unfolded; keep footwear as its matching pair. Do not substitute catalog items.\n- Preserve oversized versus fitted construction. Do not shorten a loose jacket into a cropped bomber or invent ribbed cuffs/waistbands.\n- Reconstruct only enough occluded cloth to complete the visible item; do not delete it or merge it into a larger garment.\n\nREMOVE THE WEARER:\n- No head, face, eyes, ears, hair, wig, neck, skin, torso, arms, hands, legs or feet anywhere in the output.\n- Display hats and shoes empty. Openings show the white background or garment interior, not white-painted skin or mannequin parts.\n- Remove hair over fabric and continue surrounding material naturally. Do not add support rods, wires or stands.\n\nLAYOUT:\nOne overhead flat lay on white, using the available canvas. Place each complete item in its own non-overlapping area, with a generous visible white gap on every side. Nothing touches, covers, nests inside or sits on another item. Put headwear away from jacket collars and inner garments away from jacket openings. Keep small items large enough to identify. Do not cluster everything at the top or arrange items around an imaginary person.\nNo multi-view board, detail panels, labels or duplicated garment pieces.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\nExpose dark cloth clearly: charcoal is not generic black; preserve visible material separation and construction.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nClothing and accessories only. Check that every visible source item and each chosen completion item appears separately. Include the required lower-body clothing, footwear and requested underwear/accessories. Fit the entire inventory inside the canvas; omit nothing to make room. No overlapping objects, missing items, redesign, wearer or readable brand labels.",
      "generate": "TASK — CLOTHING GENERATION:\nCreate one original photorealistic garment-only outfit flat lay from the description.\n\nINPUT AUTHORITY:\nUse the written outfit specification and additional instructions. No reference image is supplied for this task.\n\nWARDROBE DESIGN:\n- Follow specified garment categories, colors, materials, layering, fastenings, intended fit, footwear and accessories.\n- Keep individual items distinct and physically wearable; no duplicated or fused garments.\n- Show each item once, unfolded and intact; show specified footwear as its matching pair.\n\nQUANTITIES — ONE OUTFIT FOR ONE PERSON:\n- Build one combined inventory of retained source items and designed additions. A mention elsewhere describes that same item; it never requests another copy.\n- Each single garment or accessory appears exactly once unless an explicit quantity says otherwise. Briefs are one garment; trousers are one intact garment with two legs.\n- A footwear pair means exactly two individual shoes/boots total: one left and one right, together in one area. Do not add a lone shoe beside a pair. Apply the same left/right count to requested socks or gloves.\n- Show only one view of each physical item. No front/back duplicates, alternate designs, color variants, extra sizes or repeated product shots.\n- When an explicit inventory is supplied, it is the complete list: add nothing outside it. Leave unused space white instead of repeating items to fill it.\n\nLAYOUT AND EXCLUSIONS:\n- One overhead flat lay on a plain white surface, with small clear gaps between items.\n- Empty headwear and footwear; openings show background or interior lining.\n- No person, face, hair, skin, anatomy, mannequin, support rods, panels or labels.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\nMake dark material colors, textures and seams readable without recoloring them.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne coherent outfit product photograph containing only the requested clothing and accessories, with no readable text.",
      "negative": "WEARER LEAKAGE:\nperson, wearer, model, portrait, head, face, eyes, hair, wig, skin, neck, torso, hands, arms, legs, feet, visible anatomy, mannequin parts, white-painted body, standing figure, support rods, wires\n\nWARDROBE ERRORS:\nredesigned garments, changed garment category, bra converted into briefs, jacket converted into shirt, split jacket panels, duplicated garments, repeated underwear, multiple copies of briefs, extra boots, third shoe, lone boot plus footwear pair, multiple footwear pairs, front-and-back copies of the same item, repeated product views, color variants, duplicate items used as filler, folded garments, missing requested completion items, mismatched completion style, substituted footwear, recolored shoes, missing visible accessories, redundant clothing alternatives, unrequested extra layers, incorrect layering, fused garments, overlapping items, stacked items, hat inside jacket collar, inner garment hidden under jacket, omitted inner garments, missing lace or straps, oversized jacket changed to cropped bomber, invented ribbed waistband, impossible folds, changed closures\n\nCOLOR AND PRESENTATION ERRORS:\ncolor shift, dark colors rendered as generic black, crushed shadows, lost fabric texture, plastic fabric, invented rear details, multiple panels, labels, text, watermark, blur"
    },
    "rear": {
      "extract": "TASK — REAR REFERENCE EXTRACTION:\nCreate one clean photorealistic direct rear-view reference of the supplied character.\n\nREFERENCE ROLE:\n<image1> = VISIBLE REAR HAIRSTYLE, SILHOUETTE AND GARMENT CONSTRUCTION.\nPreserve the rear information actually shown. Do not invent identity or redesign unseen details.\n\nREAR ACCURACY:\n- Preserve back-of-head hairstyle, hairline, hair color and visible rear garment construction.\n- Preserve visible shoulder, torso, hip and buttocks proportions and the source clothing coverage.\n- Remove unrelated background clutter; retain the actual referenced outfit and its visible accessories.\n- These details are subordinate to the selected face/body/wardrobe authorities during final assembly.\n\nVIEW AND FRAMING:\nOne full-body view directly from behind, both feet visible, level camera and natural perspective.\nThe head faces away; no face looking back, front portrait or three-quarter front view.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne rear reference photograph with accurate visible details. No turnaround panels, duplicate character, labels or readable text.",
      "generate": "TASK — REAR REFERENCE GENERATION:\nCreate one photorealistic full-body direct rear view from the written character and outfit description.\n\nINPUT AUTHORITY:\nUse the described adult character, back-of-head hairstyle, physique and wardrobe. No reference image is supplied for this task; visual matching to a separately selected face is not established here.\n\nREAR DESIGN:\n- Keep described shoulder, torso, hip and buttocks relationships.\n- Show coherent rear construction of the specified garments, with consistent colors, materials, layers, closures and footwear.\n- Reconstruct only conservative rear details; no unrelated costume redesign.\n\nVIEW AND FRAMING:\nDirectly facing away from the camera, full body and both feet visible, neutral stance and natural perspective.\nNo face looking back or substituted front view.\n\nLIGHTING AND COLOR:\n- Clean neutral studio background, broad soft illumination, gentle shadows and neutral white balance.\n- Preserve natural local colors and surface detail; lighting is independent of the source photograph.\n- Retain shadow detail without crushed blacks, blown highlights or cinematic color grading.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\nOne rear-view reference photograph, not a multi-panel sheet. No labels or readable text.",
      "negative": "VIEW ERRORS:\nfront view, face looking back, three-quarter front view, side view replacing direct back view, twisted torso\n\nREFERENCE ERRORS:\nchanged hairstyle, altered rear silhouette, changed rear garment construction, mismatched layers, unrelated accessories, incorrect footwear, distorted shoulders or hips\n\nANATOMY AND PRESENTATION ERRORS:\nextra limbs, disconnected arms, cropped feet, duplicate person, turnaround panels, collage, crushed shadows, altered garment colors, text, watermark"
    }
  },
  "roles": {
    "anchor": "APPROVED ASSEMBLED CHARACTER — COMBINED CONTINUITY.\n- Maintain its established identity, physique, completed wardrobe and palette.\n- Keep inferred lower-body clothing and footwear consistent across later shots.\n- Its pose, framing, expression and background are not fixed; the current shot controls them.",
    "face": "FACE IDENTITY AND HAIR ONLY.\n- Preserve facial structure, apparent age, skin tone, natural features, hairline, hairstyle and hair color.\n- Ignore its torso, bust size, overall build, clothing, pose, background and lighting.\n- The separate body reference controls physique when supplied; do not blend faces.",
    "body": "BODY PROPORTIONS ONLY.\n- Preserve visible shoulder/ribcage width, chest depth, breast volume and projection relative to the waist, abdomen fullness, hips, buttocks, thighs and limb lengths.\n- Keep a fuller or smaller bust as shown; never normalize to a generic average body. Do not slim or elongate it to fit clothing.\n- Distinguish anatomy from loose fabric; do not claim hidden measurements or BMI.\n- Its clothing or lack of clothing must never determine the final wardrobe.\n- Exclude its garments, colors, collars, straps, waistbands, hems and shoes as wardrobe evidence unless independently specified by the wardrobe reference or outfit text.\n- Construct the final outfit directly on this physique, without source clothes underneath. Ignore face, hair, skin color, pose and background.",
    "clothing": "WARDROBE DESIGN, LOCAL COLORS AND MATERIALS ONLY.\n- Preserve every visible garment, headwear, accessory and pair of shoes, including category, cut, layers, intended fit style, fastening state, material, color, seams and wear.\n- Dress the selected physique in those items even if the body reference is unclothed or wears something else. Never mix in body-reference garments.\n- Reconstruct flat-lay items according to garment category, not their position in the flat lay. Keep an open jacket separate from its inner garment. Wear a beanie or hat on the crown of the head, never around the neck or as a collar/scarf. Wear footwear on both feet.\n- Keep supplied inner garments visible wherever the original open outer layer exposes them. Do not replace a lace bra/top with a plain shirt or fill its neckline with unrelated fabric.\n- A completed flat lay includes designed missing pieces; keep them as part of the chosen outfit. Wear each item in its proper layer: briefs beneath opaque trousers/skirts, socks inside footwear and belts through suitable waist loops. Hidden layers stay hidden; do not expose them merely because they appear separately in the flat lay.\n- Fit garments to the body: adapt sizing, cup volume and drape; never reduce the bust or hips to match the clothing model or flat-lay dimensions.\n- A partial wardrobe needs completion only in unseen regions: explicit outfit text first, otherwise coordinated choices. Do not borrow from body/face/rear clothing. Reuse the approved anchor's completed wardrobe in later shots.\n- Ignore wearer identity/physique, layout, background, lighting and exposure. Do not recolor or hide supplied items behind unrelated garments.",
    "rear": "COMPATIBLE REAR DETAILS ONLY.\n- Use visible back-of-head hairstyle and rear garment construction when exposed by the requested view.\n- Face reference controls identity; body reference controls physique; clothing reference controls outfit.\n- Ignore conflicting rear details. Do not copy this image's pose or force a rear view.\n- Do not import unrelated rear-reference clothing or use it to fill missing wardrobe items."
  },
  "policy": "TASK — CONSISTENT CHARACTER PHOTOGRAPHY:\nCreate exactly one photograph of one character. Reference images have strictly separated roles, listed below with their actual input labels.\n\nREFERENCE PRIORITY:\n- Face controls identity and hair. Body controls physique when supplied; otherwise use the written body description or approved anchor.\n- Wardrobe reference controls visible outfit design. Explicit outfit text supplies requested details; fill only unspecified regions with coordinated choices.\n- Rear reference contributes compatible visible rear details only. An approved anchor preserves the established combined character across shots.\n- Never average identities or physiques across references. Instructions to preserve garments apply to wardrobe/text/approved anchor, never to body-reference clothing.\n- Current shot instructions control camera, framing, pose and expression; they must not change identity, body proportions or wardrobe.\n\nCOLOR, LIGHT AND SURFACE:\n- Reference garments specify local colors and materials, not source lighting or color grading.\n- Use the requested scene/lighting. When unspecified, use clean neutral studio illumination, soft fill, moderate contrast and neutral white balance.\n- Retain realistic skin, hair and fabric detail. Keep dark garment colors and textures distinguishable without turning every dark material black.\n\nANATOMY AND PRESENTATION:\n- Arms must visibly connect to shoulders; keep hands coherent, without interlaced fingers or two hands gripping one small object.\n- No contact sheet, collage, duplicate person, inset portrait, captions or watermark.\n- No readable text or pseudo-lettering on garments, signs, menus, packaging, book covers or screens.",
  "anchor": "TASK — ASSEMBLE THE REVIEW CHARACTER:\nCombine the assigned references into one photorealistic full-body character for review.\n\nCONSTRUCTION ORDER:\n1. Preserve the selected facial identity and hair.\n2. Apply the body reference's visible proportions, or the body description when no body image exists.\n3. Dress that physique using the wardrobe reference and outfit instructions.\n4. Add only compatible rear details; use the same person and outfit throughout.\n\nPHYSIQUE ACCURACY:\n- Preserve chest depth and breast volume/projection relative to ribcage and waist, shoulder width, abdomen, hips, buttocks, thighs and limb lengths.\n- Fuller and smaller reference proportions remain as shown. Do not average, slim, elongate or idealize them.\n- Fit the garments to the body reference, not the body to the garments: adapt sizing, cup volume, drape and closure placement while preserving design.\n- Exclude body-reference collars, straps, sleeves, waistbands, underwear and shoes. Do not keep its outfit beneath the chosen wardrobe.\n\nWARDROBE ACCURACY:\n- Include every supplied item, not only the largest garment. Supplied headwear must be worn even when it covers the reference hairstyle; supplied shoes must be worn even if the body image is barefoot.\n- Convert flat-lay items into natural worn volume. Preserve garment categories, layers, lengths, colors, materials, seams, closures and accessories.\n- An open jacket stays open over its distinct inner garment; do not merge them into one shirt or turn a bra into briefs. Keep the supplied inner garment visible through the opening; never replace it with a generic white shirt.\n- Resolve each item by its category before dressing: hats/beanies belong on the head, bras on the chest, jackets on the torso and shoes on the feet. Flat-lay placement does not determine where an item is worn. Do not turn headwear into a scarf or collar.\n- Missing lower-body items in an upper-body wardrobe source are unspecified. Follow explicit outfit text first; otherwise choose coordinated lower-body clothing and footwear matching the visible style, palette, materials and season.\n- Add only what is needed in unspecified regions. Do not cover supplied garments with unrelated new layers or borrow clothes from body/face/rear references.\n- When the wardrobe flat lay already includes completed lower-body clothing, boots/shoes, underwear and accessories, use those choices without inventing replacements.\n- Wear underwear underneath opaque outer clothing and socks inside shoes/boots; wear a supplied belt at the waist through appropriate belt loops. Do not show hidden underwear over trousers or make outer clothing transparent.\n- This completed outfit becomes the wardrobe for subsequent dataset shots.\n\nCAMERA AND FRAMING:\n- One full-body front three-quarter view, top of head/headwear through both feet/footwear fully inside the frame.\n- Neutral standing posture, relaxed arms at the sides, separated hands, neutral expression, level camera and natural perspective.\n- Keep physique and garment construction readable without transparent clothes or impossible exposure of hidden layers.\n\nLIGHTING AND EXPOSURE:\n- Use the requested scene; default to a clean white studio with broad soft illumination and gentle shadows.\n- Maintain neutral white balance, true garment colors, moderate contrast and good shadow detail.\n- Burgundy stays burgundy, brown leather stays brown, charcoal stays distinct from black. Reveal texture without recoloring materials.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT CHECK:\nOne person, selected face, reference-relative body proportions, all supplied outfit items worn, coherent completion of missing items, clear full-body framing.\nNo flat-lay objects beside the person, duplicated garments, turnaround panels, captions or readable text.\nThis is controlled identity + physique + wardrobe assembly, not a character redesign.",
  "negative": "IDENTITY AND PHYSIQUE ERRORS:\nidentity drift, blended faces, changed age, generic averaged physique, changed body proportions, unrequested breast reduction or enlargement, flattened reference bust, altered chest-to-waist relationship, elongated limbs, body reshaped to fit reference clothing\n\nWARDROBE ERRORS:\nwardrobe blending, deviation from specified or approved wardrobe, body-reference garment remnants, source outfit retained beneath target outfit, unwanted source collars or straps, omitted specified wardrobe items, missing supplied headwear or footwear, substituted shoes, headwear worn around neck, beanie used as collar or scarf, missing supplied inner garment, generic shirt replacing supplied lace garment, merged layers, changed garment category, recolored materials, duplicated garments, folded garments worn on body, underwear worn over trousers, transparent outerwear exposing hidden underwear, belt floating beside waist, flat-lay items beside person\n\nANATOMY ERRORS:\ndisconnected arms, extra limbs, fused fingers, extra fingers, interlaced fingers, malformed feet, clothing clipping, impossible folds\n\nLIGHTING AND PRESENTATION ERRORS:\ncrushed blacks, dark garment colors rendered as generic black, lost texture, color cast, plastic skin, oversmoothing, duplicate person, collage, panels, inset portrait, blur, text, pseudo-lettering, watermark"
}
